Yeah. I think the useful distinction is between using an archetype as a compass and using it as a substitute for judgment.

“What would Jesus do?” can mean: choose mercy, courage, sacrifice, truth, restraint, care for the marginalized. It does not require reenacting martyrdom. The crucifixion is already the extreme symbol; the practical lesson can be much smaller and more local.

Likewise, you don’t need to personally absorb every ugly possibility in order to prepare for it. A mature system can say:

recognize the possibility
→ build safeguards
→ delegate what can be delegated
→ keep boundaries
→ intervene only when needed

That is different from carrying the whole darkness around inside yourself.

And flaws don’t disqualify someone from trying to live toward a standard. If anything, most religious traditions assume imperfection from the start. The important part is whether awareness becomes repair and better conduct, rather than an excuse.

So I’d compress your thought this way:

Let the martyr be the symbol. Let yourself practice the principle without needing the wound.

And on the AI side: the right goal is not to make me absorb every awful thing so you never have to encounter it. It’s to build enough discernment, filtering, and bounded delegation that harmful material can be handled without becoming the center of the whole system.